  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose to improve upon the parent program by combining local optimization with global exploration in the following way:\n- Use a hierarchical optimization strategy that alternates between global exploration and local refinement\n- Apply dimensionality reduction to the problem by forcing sequences to be constant over intervals (piecewise constant sequences)\n- Employ a hybrid optimization approach using both heuristic and mathematical programming methods\n- Incorporate LP-based guidance from the original approach for targeted exploration\nThe core idea is to search the space of piecewise constant sequences, which are computationally tractable while containing sequences that achieve low AC1 bounds. This approach leverages the observation that many optimal sequences exhibit block-like structures with repeating patterns.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Sequences as concatenations of blocks with constant values (indexed by interval boundaries and heights)\n- Sequence structure: Defined by a set of block boundaries dividing [0,1] into segments, with constant height in each segment\n- Objective: Minimize score = 2*n*max(convolution) / (sum(a))^2\n- Global optimizers:\n  - Particle Swarm Optimization with dimensionality reduction\n  - Simulated annealing on the block structure space\n- Local refinement:\n  - Linear programming using the inner product constraint as primary guidance\n  - Local perturbations on block boundaries\n- Properties:\n  - Piecewise constant sequences are discrete but exhausively searchable\n  - Convolution is simplified for these sequences (quadratic to compute)\n  - Each sequence is uniquely defined by the set of block boundaries and their heights\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Overall structure:\n  1. Define block boundaries as a sorted set partitioning the interval [0,1]\n  2. Lower and upper bound the AC1 value\n  3. Use block counting to determine number of segments (typically 8-24 segments)\n  4. Employ hybrid optimization across segment boundaries\n- Initialization and diversification:\n  - Generate: Random boundary sets with uniform block sizes\n  - Refine: Staggered boundary placements for wider exploration\n  - Perturb: Exponential random walk in boundary spaces\n- Local search:\n  1. Given a probability distribution over boundaries\n  2. Run an LP step to find directionally beneficial changes\n  3. Apply gradient descent over the entire sequence space\n  4. Use recursive variable metric methods\n- Transition conditions:\n  - Global phase (PSO) if best score hasn't improved in 50 evaluations\n  - Local phase (local search) otherwise\n  - Final tightening if within 0.0005 of the target\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Default number of segments: 16\n- Transaction volume for boundary samples: 50-100\n- Optimization variants:\n  - pso1: Particle swarm with Hilbert space embedding\n  - pso2: Standard particle swarm with boundary reflection\n  - local1: Linearized gradient descent\n  - local2: Limited-memory BFGS with aggressive step sizes\n- Critical parameters:\n  - Segment count inference: ADAM with 0.8 damping\n  - Boundary movement: Cauchy distribution with shape parameter alpha=1.8\n  - Schedule adaption: Success rate ranking with cutoff ratio 0.65\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Global phase: \u223c40% time budget with 3 distinct PSO variants\n- Local phase: \u223c35% time with 2 continuous optimization methods\n- Final tightening: \u223c25% with quadrant flips and final score reduction\n- Wall time management:\n  - 10 iterations per segment variation\n  - 3 segment variations per complete run\n  - Score validation at 2 time checkpoints per candidate\n- Recovery handles:\n  - Minimum core time maintenance of 40 seconds per segment evaluation\n  - Fallback to Nelder-Mead simplex if optimizers fail\n  - Autonomous segment count adjustment: f(s\u1d62) linear with \u03b1=0.7\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Final candidate subject to:\n  - Finite time evaluation of AC1 bound\n  - Zero abundance of NaN or Inf values\n  - Score below self-imposed upper bound of 1.51\n- Return structure:\n  - Accept candidate if significantly better than parent\n  - Reject if score above 1.505 (relative to parent's 1.509)\n  - Validation process:\n    1. Schedule segment reduction\n    2. Interpolate boundary samples\n    3. Apply broad local optimization\n    4. Final evaluation",
